{"t":1794734604,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"624ace50-19d9-452d-97aa-4b7d0fea10d5","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-15T07:58:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 150758\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n258 AM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nMade some changes to decrease RH late Tuesday going into Tuesday\nnight and Wednesday. Also, wrote a new fire weather discussion\nas well. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Colder than normal through Monday with gradually\n  diminishing winds and clouds. \n\n- 2) Dry conditions persist through the upcoming week with \n  increasing temperatures starting Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder than normal through Monday with gradually\ndiminishing winds and clouds.\n\nRegional composite reflectivity still depicts shower activity \nwell off to the northwest. Latest runs of the HRRR still depict\nthat some of this activity makes it into the typical upslope\nareas out west later this morning and intermittently throughout\nthe day today. Little to no measurable precipitation is \nexpected for the majority of the area, but a dusting of snow is \npossible for west-facing slopes above 1900 feet along and west \nof the Allegheny Front, with spotty 1 to 2 inch amounts possible\nabove about 2700 feet if some of the heavier streamers make it \nthis far south. An SPS may be needed at a future time, but will\nwait to see something a bit more organized before going that\nroute. \n\nOf note, the 14/12Z REFS had a few members with several\nhundredths of an inch of QPF from a streamer over the eastern WV\npanhandle, north-central MD and into extreme northern VA tonight\nthrough Sunday. The 00z REFS did come down a bit with this\nactivity, but there is still some hint that there could be some\ncarryover between the Allegheny mountains and the Blue Ridge\nperhaps. Forecast soundings depict steep low-level lapse\nrates and moisture at 3-5 kft, which will lead to waves of \nstratocumulus along with the potential for some sprinkles or\nflurries throughout the afternoon. This would likely be in the \nform of rain for most, but can't totally discount the idea of a \nsteadier shower (or perhaps a light dusting of snow above about \n700-800 feet) through Sunday evening. Kept sprinkles/flurries in\nthe forecast for now to depict this threat, as the \nprobabilities still weren't high enough to go with slight chance\nPOPs at this time. \n\nWind gusts will gradually subside through Monday, but remain\nsomewhat elevated today (albeit less than previous days). Gusts\nwill be slower to subside at higher elevations (especially at \nnight compared to the lower elevations). Clouds will also \ngradually thin and scatter out during this period.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions persist through the upcoming \nweek with increasing temperatures starting Tuesday.\n\nWinds continue to diminish on Tuesday and remain light through \nthe end of the week as high pressure transitions overhead in the\nwake of the front. As upper-level ridging continues to build \nover several days, dry conditions are expected to persist \nthrough the end of the forecast period with mostly clear skies \noverhead and progressively warming temperatures as the week goes\non. Afternoon highs could begin to approach the upper 60s \nbetween Thursday and Friday, well above seasonable normals, with\novernight lows generally in the 40s. No precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated Tuesday through Friday.\n\nLooking ahead to the end of next week, an upper-level trough \ncould develop over the Great Lakes and produce a stemming cold \nfront in the days following; will continue to monitor this \npattern for any precipitation potential over the coming days.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nOther than a spotty flurry or sprinkle today, it will be dry for\nall terminals through early next week. Wind gusts to around 20 \nknots are anticipated today, and some 10-15 knot gusts may even\nlinger into tonight. By Monday, winds taper off a little bit\nmore, before becoming much lighter by Monday night. \n\nNW winds stay below 10 knots Tuesday through Thursday as high \npressure remains overhead. VFR conditions are anticipated across \nterminals through this period, with mostly clear skies persisting \nthrough the midweek. Winds shift southwesterly on Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNorth to northwest winds remain elevated today across all\nwaters, with gusts around 20 to 25 knots expected. SCAs remain\nin effect through this evening for all waters, and have been\nextended overnight in southern portions of the waters. Winds\nwill taper off into Monday, but SCAs may still need to be\nextended at least for a portion of the day before that occurs.\nConfidence wasn't quite high enough at this point for that\nhowever, so that would come at a later time. \n\nWinds stay below SCA thresholds Monday night through Thursday, \nshifting from northwesterly to southwesterly by Wednesday. No \nprecipitation is anticipated during this period as high pressure\nremains overhead.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nRemaining brisk today as cool high pressure builds towards the\nregion and a departing low remains well to our northeast. Aside\nfrom some intermittent mountain snow showers along and west of the\nAllegheny Front, dry conditions are expected for most. Brisk \nwinds will continue through Monday with waves of clouds, but the\nmagnitude will be less than recent days. \n\nHigh pressure gradually builds overhead into the middle of next \nweek, then temperatures will begin to warm mid to late next week.\nDuring this timeframe, while RH winds will diminish, RH values\nwill go down substantially. The dry air really builds in starting\nTuesday into Tuesday night, and looks to continue for several days\nthereafter. Overnight recoveries, especially on the ridges, will \nlikely be poor as well starting Tuesday night. Expect afternoon RH\nvalues to even drop well into the 20s each day. This will also be\npaired with seasonably warm temperatures, so this will really act\nto dry out already very dry fuels. \n\nNot helping matters is that there is no precipitation expected \neast of the Appalachians through at least the end of next week. \nGiven the setup over the next week, will really be watching for \nany potential dry frontal passages in the long range as the next \ndays to really watch out for. \n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EST this evening for ANZ530-\n     531-535-536-538-539.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 AM EST Monday for ANZ532>534-537-\n     540>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CJL/SRT\nAVIATION...CJL/SRT\nMARINE...CJL/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794734527,"days":{"2026-11-15":{"hi":55,"lo":36,"pop":8,"short":"Scattered Rain And Snow Showers then Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-16":{"hi":57,"lo":36,"pop":2,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-17":{"hi":61,"lo":35,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-18":{"hi":65,"lo":37,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-19":{"hi":66,"lo":41,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-20":{"hi":68,"lo":42,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-21":{"hi":67,"lo":null,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794733200,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,4500,5200,5800,6300,6800,7300,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[8,8,8,6,7,8,8,9,9,9,9,9,8,7,6,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,5,6,7,7,7,6,6,6,5,4,3,3,2,2,2,2,3,3,3,3],